  <p>How your morning goes sets the tone for your whole study day. Grab the phone in bed and you have handed your attention away before your feet touch the floor. A short, deliberate routine does the opposite: it hands the day back to you.</p>

  <h2 id="why">Why mornings matter</h2>
  <p>Early daylight helps set your body clock, which research links to better sleep and mood (Blume and colleagues), and light movement sharpens attention for the hours that follow (Hillman). You do not need an elaborate wellness ritual. A few small, reliable actions in the first half-hour change how the rest of the day feels.</p>

  <h2 id="routine">A simple routine</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Get up at a consistent time</strong>, close to your schooldays even at weekends.</li>
    <li><strong>Daylight and a little movement:</strong> open the curtains, step outside, or stretch for a few minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>Water first</strong>, before caffeine.</li>
    <li><strong>Glance at your plan</strong> for the day in the <a href="/tools/timetable-maker/">timetable maker</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Start your first planned task</strong> before the day fills up.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="phone">The phone rule</h2>
  <p>The single highest-value change is delaying the phone. Give yourself the first hour phone-free so messages, feeds and notifications do not set your mood and hijack your attention before you have chosen your own first move. Charge it outside the bedroom so reaching it takes effort. More on this in <a href="/guides/how-to-avoid-distractions-while-studying/">how to avoid distractions while studying</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="firsttask">Choose the first task</h2>
  <p>Decide the night before what you will do first, and make it concrete and small. Starting with a clear, planned task means momentum instead of drift. Set that task with the <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> and run it as one focused <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">Pomodoro</a> to get moving. Lally's habit research is clear that repetition at a consistent time is what makes the whole routine stick. To build the rest of the day around it, see <a href="/guides/how-to-build-a-daily-study-routine/">how to build a daily study routine</a>.</p>
</Guide>
